EXPERIMENT 8 – STRING REVERSAL USING JUNIT

AIM
To write JUnit test cases to reverse a given string and validate the output using JUnit assertions.
ALGORITHM
1. Create a Maven project and add the JUnit 5 dependency.
2. Create a class named StringUtil.
3. Write the reverse() method using StringBuilder.
4. Create StringUtilTest with different string inputs.
5. Use assertEquals(), assertNotNull() and assertNull() to verify results.
6. Run the tests using mvn test.
CODE
StringUtil.java
public class StringUtil {
    public static String reverse(String s) {
        if (s == null) {
            return null;
        }
        return new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString();
    }
}


StringUtilTest.java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class StringUtilTest {
    @Test
    void reverseOfWord() {
        String result = StringUtil.reverse("hello");
        assertNotNull(result);
        assertEquals("olleh", result);
    }

    @Test
    void emptyStringAndPalindrome() {
        assertEquals("", StringUtil.reverse(""));
        assertEquals("madam", StringUtil.reverse("madam"));
    }

    @Test
    void nullInputReturnsNull() {
        assertNull(StringUtil.reverse(null));
    }
}


INPUT
Input	Expected Output
hello	olleh
Empty string	Empty string
madam	madam
